<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- Entitlements for NeoDropFinder.appex: the App Sandbox, and nothing else.

     The App Sandbox is not a choice here. PlugInKit refuses to register an extension without it — the
     refusal is a line in the system log ("plug-ins must be sandboxed") and nothing else: `pluginkit -m
     -p com.apple.FinderSync` simply never lists it, and Finder shows no badges and no error. That is
     what a sandboxed extension buys and what it costs:

       * the extension can read nothing outside its own container;
       * the app publishes the badge index into that container from outside — the only channel the two
         processes need — which works when both bundles carry the same team identity and is refused
         otherwise: an ad-hoc build badges nothing at all (macOS 14+ protects a container from other
         teams). No App Group is declared here either; see packaging/entitlements.plist for the
         measurements behind both decisions, and AGENTS.md for the log lines.

     Deliberately absent: any file access. The extension never opens the user's files — Finder hands it
     URLs and the index hands it badge identifiers, and both are just strings — so it needs no
     user-selected, home-relative or network entitlement, and adding one would widen what a compromised
     extension could reach for no benefit. scripts/verify.sh checks that the sandbox is declared and
     that an App Group has not been added back: it cannot work from a non-sandboxed app. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
